 "configuration": {
  "baseline": "production configuration of scripts/train_xgb.py (6 lags, PARAMS, 89 features), seeds 0 1 2; seed 0 reproduces best_iteration 2151 and validation RMSE 2.6565 of reports/xgb_baseline.json",
  "v1": "scripts/train_xgb.py --dst-skip 1: build_features(dst_skip=1), every Dst feature (dst_lag0..5, dst_diff1, dst_diff3, dst_min24, dst_mean24) built from dst.shift(1), solar wind and calendar features unchanged, target Dst(t+1), the model learns the change from Dst(t-1); seeds 0 1 2",
  "v2k1": "scripts/train_xgb.py --dst-newest-noise 1: the clean train years plus 1 copy whose Dst(t), as seen from row t only, carries an AR(1) error (mean -3 nT, standard deviation 4 nT, coefficient 0.6, oculstorm.augment.newest_hour_error, seed 1); dst_lag0, dst_diff1, dst_diff3, dst_min24 and dst_mean24 of the row see the error, older lags and the target are clean; seeds 0 1 2",
  "v2k2": "--dst-newest-noise 2: as v2k1 plus a second copy with half the mean and half the standard deviation (seed 2)",
  "early_stopping": "on the clean validation years for every variant, as in production",
  "perturbed_validation": "validation features rebuilt with the same error recipe at full level and a fixed seed (12345) on the newest hour of every row, target clean; the realised error on the validation hours is in perturbation_on_validation. V1 does not read Dst(t), so its perturbed scores equal its clean scores by construction. A Gaussian AR(1) puts 14.9% of hours within 1 nT; the live sample in the issue had 25%, so the copy is somewhat harsher than the live errors it imitates.",
  "references": "clean set: persistence from Dst(t) and from Dst(t-1) (2 hour persistence); perturbed set: persistence from the perturbed Dst(t) and from the clean Dst(t-1)",
  "regimes": "by the clean observed Dst at the target hour",
  "not_run": "GRU: scripts/train_gru.py has no validation only mode and computes test_rmse in a normal run, so it was skipped; build_windows has no dst_skip. --ring-current is refused together with either option.",
